ion\": \"Expected mean squares are integral to the analysis of mixed models, providing a basis for assessing variance components associated with different sources of variation. This concept is particularly relevant in two-factor and three-factor interaction models, where understanding the interplay between multiple factors is crucial. The application of expected mean squares facilitates the identification of significant effects in complex experimental designs, enhancing the interpretability of results [Data: Entities (2630, 2632); Relationships (3690, 3699)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Applications of Mixed Models\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Mixed models are widely used in various research fields, including agriculture, psychology, and clinical trials, where data often exhibit both fixed and random variability. These models allow researchers to account for unobserved heterogeneity and provide more accurate estimates of fixed effects. The ability to handle complex data structures makes mixed models a powerful tool for researchers aiming to generalize findings beyond specific experimental conditions [Data: Entities (141, 2624); Relationships (3660)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Complexity and Challenges of Mixed Models\",\n            \"explanation\": \"While mixed models offer significant advantages in data analysis, they also present challenges, particularly in model specification and interpretation. The complexity of these models can lead to difficulties in understanding the relationships between fixed and random effects, especially for non-statisticians. Additionally, ensuring that the assumptions of normality and independence are met is crucial for valid results, which can complicate the analysis process [Data: Entities (141, 2624); Relationships (3660)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Importance of Software Implementation\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The implementation of statistical models, including mixed models and ANOVA, is facilitated by various statistical software packages such as R, SAS, and SPSS. These tools provide researchers with the necessary functionalities to fit models, conduct hypothesis tests, and perform diagnostics. The accessibility of these software solutions has democratized advanced statistical analysis, allowing a broader range of researchers to apply complex methodologies in their work [Data: Entities (141, 2624); Relationships (3660)].\"\n        }\n    ]\n}", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": [], "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null}, "content_filter_results": {"hate": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "protected_material_code": {"filtered": false, "detected": false}, "protected_material_text": {"filtered": false, "detected": false}, "self_harm": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "sexual": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "violence": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}}}], "created": 1768463756, "model": "gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18", "object": "chat.completion", "service_tier": null, "system_fingerprint": "fp_f97eff32c5", "usage": {"completion_tokens": 1115, "prompt_tokens": 11303, "total_tokens": 12418, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": 0, "audio_tokens": 0, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "rejected_prediction_tokens": 0}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": 0, "cached_tokens": 1024}}, "prompt_filter_results": [{"prompt_index": 0, "content_filter_results": {"hate": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "jailbreak": {"filtered": false, "detected": false}, "self_harm": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "sexual": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "violence": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}}}]}, "input": {"messages": [{"content": "\nYou are an AI assistant that helps a human analyst to perform general information discovery. Information discovery is the process of identifying and assessing relevant information associated with certain entities (e.g., organizations and individuals) within a network.\n\n# Goal\nWrite a comprehensive report of a community, given a list of entities that belong to the community as well as their relationships and optional associated claims. The report will be used to inform decision-makers about information associated with the community and their potential impact. The content of this report includes an overview of the community's key entities, their legal compliance, technical capabilities, reputation, and noteworthy claims.\n\n# Report Structure\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its entities.\n- IMPACT SEVERITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation of the IMPACT severity rating.\n- DETAILED FINDINGS: A list of 5-10 key insights about the community. Each insight should have a short summary followed by multiple paragraphs of explanatory text grounded according to the grounding rules below. Be comprehensive.\n\nReturn output as a well-formed JSON-formatted string with the following format:\n    {\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\n# Example Input\n-----------\nText:\n\nEntities\n\nid,entity,description\n5,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n6,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is an organization that is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n\nRelationships\n\nid,source,target,description\n37,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n38,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n39,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,The Unity March is taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n40,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,TRIBUNE SPOTLIGHT,Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity march taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n41,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,BAILEY ASADI,Bailey Asadi is speaking at Verdant Oasis Plaza about the march\n43,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,UNITY MARCH,Harmony Assembly is organizing the Unity March\n\nOutput:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza and Unity March\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community revolves around the Verdant Oasis Plaza, which is the location of the Unity March. The plaza has relationships with the Harmony Assembly, Unity March, and Tribune Spotlight, all of which are associated with the march event.\",\n    \"rating\": 5.0,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate due to the potential for unrest or conflict during the Unity March.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza as the central location\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza is the central entity in this community, serving as the location for the Unity March. This plaza is the common link between all other entities, suggesting its significance in the community. The plaza's association with the march could potentially lead to issues such as public disorder or conflict, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. [Data: Entities (5), Relationships (37, 38, 39, 40, 41,+more)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Harmony Assembly's role in the community\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Harmony Assembly is another key entity in this community, being the organizer of the march at Verdant Oasis Plaza. The nature of Harmony Assembly and its march could be a potential source of threat, depending on their objectives and the reactions they provoke. The relationship between Harmony Assembly and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Entities(6), Relationships (38, 43)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Unity March as a significant event\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Unity March is a significant event taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza. This event is a key factor in the community's dynamics and could be a potential source of threat, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. The relationship between the march and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Relationships (39)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Role of Tribune Spotlight\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity March taking place in Verdant Oasis Plaza. This suggests that the event has attracted media attention, which could amplify its impact on the community. The role of Tribune Spotlight could be significant in shaping public perception of the event and the entities involved. [Data: Relationships (40)]\"\n        }\n    ]\n}\n\n\n# Real Data\n\nUse the following text for your answer. Do not make anything up in your answer.\n\nText:\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n2624,RANDOM EFFECT,\"### Comprehensive Summary of Random Effects\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nA random effect is a variable in a statistical model that introduces random variability into the model, allowing for individual differences in the response. It is used to account for variations that are not explained by fixed effects, thereby enabling the modeling of variability across different levels of a factor. Random effects are particularly useful in hierarchical or nested data structures, where observations are grouped within higher-level units.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure**  \nIn a mixed-effects model, the structure can be represented as:\n\\[ Y_{ij} = \\mu + \\beta X_{ij} + u_{j} + \\epsilon_{ij} \\]\nwhere:\n- \\( Y_{ij} \\) is the response variable for the \\( i \\)-th observation in the \\( j \\)-th group,\n- \\( \\mu \\) is the overall mean,\n- \\( \\beta X_{ij} \\) represents the fixed effects,\n- \\( u_{j} \\) is the random effect associated with the \\( j \\)-th group (assumed to be normally distributed with mean 0 and variance \\( \\sigma^2_u \\)),\n- \\( \\epsilon_{ij} \\) is the residual error (also assumed to be normally distributed with mean 0 and variance \\( \\sigma^2 \\)).\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nRandom effects are typically incorporated into experimental designs that involve hierarchical or nested structures, such as:\n- Randomized block designs,\n- Mixed models where both fixed and random effects are present,\n- Longitudinal studies where repeated measures are taken on the same subjects.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nThe validity of models incorporating random effects relies on several assumptions:\n1. The random effects are normally distributed.\n2. The random effects are independent of the residual errors.\n3. The levels of the random effects are sampled from a larger population.\n4. The variance components associated with random effects are constant across groups.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nRandom effects should be used when:\n- The data structure is hierarchical or nested.\n- There is a need to account for variability between groups or clusters.\n- The researcher is interested in generalizing findings beyond the specific levels of the random effects included in the study.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis of models with random effects typically involves:\n1. Specification of the mixed model.\n2. Estimation of parameters using methods such as Restricted Maximum Likelihood (REML) or Maximum Likelihood (ML).\n3. Hypothesis testing for fixed effects and variance components using likelihood ratio tests or Wald tests.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \n- Random effects models can handle complex data structures and account for unobserved heterogeneity.\n- They provide more accurate estimates of fixed effects by appropriately modeling the variability among groups.\n- They allow for the generalization of results to a larger population from which the random effects are drawn.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \n- The complexity of random effects models can lead to difficulties in interpretation.\n- Estimation can be computationally intensive, especially with large datasets or complex models.\n- Mis-specification of the random effects structure can lead to biased results.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nRandom effects are often discussed in conjunction with:\n- Fixed effects models,\n- Hierarchical linear models,\n- Generalized linear mixed models (GLMMs),\n- Bayesian hierarchical models.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing random effects models, researchers should consider:\n- The appropriate selection of random effects based on the study design.\n- The need for sufficient data at each level of the random effect to ensure reliable estimates.\n- The potential for overfitting if too many random effects are included.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nCommon statistical software packages that facilitate the analysis of random effects include:\n- R (using packages such as `lme4` and `nlme`),\n- SAS (using PROC MIXED),\n- SPSS (using Mixed Models procedures).\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nRandom effects models are widely used in various fields, including:\n- Psychology (to analyze data from repeated measures designs),\n- Ecology (to model species abundance across different sites),\n- Medicine (to evaluate treatment effects in clinical trials with multiple sites or subjects).\n\nIn summary, random effects are a crucial component of mixed models that allow researchers to account for variability across different levels of a factor, enhancing the robustness and generalizability of statistical analyses.\",3\r\n2632,FIXED EFFECTS,\"### Summary of Fixed Effects\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nFixed effects are factors in an experimental design that are held constant and do not vary across experimental units. In the context of statistical modeling, fixed effects refer to the effects that are assumed to be the same for all individuals or experimental units within the study. The primary purpose of incorporating fixed effects in a model is to estimate the average effect of these factors on the response variable, allowing researchers to understand the influence of specific treatments or conditions.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure**  \nIn a typical linear model, fixed effects can be represented as follows:\n\n\\[ Y_{ij} = \\mu + \\alpha_i + \\epsilon_{ij} \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( Y_{ij} \\) is the response variable for the \\( j \\)-th observation in the \\( i \\)-th treatment group,\n- \\( \\mu \\) is the overall mean,\n- \\( \\alpha_i \\) represents the fixed effect of the \\( i \\)-th factor level,\n- \\( \\epsilon_{ij} \\) is the random error term, assumed to be normally distributed with mean zero and constant variance.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nIn experimental designs involving fixed effects, the layout typically includes a set of treatment levels that are systematically applied across all experimental units. For example, in a completely randomized design, each treatment is randomly assigned to experimental units, ensuring that the fixed effects are consistently applied.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nThe validity of fixed effects models relies on several assumptions:\n1. Independence of observations: The response variable measurements are independent of one another.\n2. Normality: The residuals (errors) of the model are normally distributed.\n3. Homoscedasticity: The variance of the residuals is constant across all levels of the fixed effects.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nFixed effects models are particularly applicable when the researcher is interested in estimating the effects of specific treatments or conditions that are not expected to vary across the population. They are suitable for controlled experiments where the levels of the factors are predetermined and fixed.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis of fixed effects typically involves the use of Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) to determine whether there are statistically significant differences among the means of the treatment groups. The F-test is commonly employed to assess the significance of the fixed effects.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \n- Fixed effects models provide clear estimates of treatment effects, making them easy to interpret.\n- They are robust to certain violations of assumptions, particularly when the focus is on estimating fixed effects rather than generalizing to a broader population.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \n- Fixed effects models do not account for random variation among experimental units, which may lead to biased estimates if there are unobserved random effects.\n- They are not suitable for situations where the levels of the factors are random or where the researcher wishes to generalize findings beyond the specific levels tested.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nRelated methods include random effects models, which account for variability among experimental units, and mixed-effects models, which incorporate both fixed and random effects. These alternatives are useful when the researcher is interested in generalizing findings or when there is inherent variability among units.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing fixed effects models, researchers should ensure that the experimental design is appropriately structured to control for confounding variables. Careful consideration of sample size is also important to achieve adequate power for detecting treatment effects.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nFixed effects models can be implemented using various statistical software packages, including R (using the `lm()` function for linear models), SAS, and SPSS. These tools provide built-in functions for conducting ANOVA and estimating fixed effects.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nFixed effects models are commonly used in agricultural experiments, clinical trials, and industrial studies where specific treatments are applied to controlled groups. For instance, a study examining the effect of different fertilizers on crop yield would utilize fixed effects to estimate the impact of each fertilizer type while controlling for other variables.\",2\r\n1012,BENNETT AND FRANKLIN,\"**Bennett and Franklin: Comprehensive Summary**\n\nBennett and Franklin are recognized authors and researchers who have made significant contributions to the field of statistical methods and experimental design, particularly in the context of random effects models and finite population cases. Their work is pivotal in understanding how to appropriately model and analyze data that arise from complex experimental designs.\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nBennett and Franklin's contributions primarily focus on the application of random effects models in the analysis of variance (ANOVA) frameworks, particularly when dealing with finite populations. Their research aims to enhance the understanding of variability within and between experimental units, thereby improving the accuracy and reliability of statistical inferences drawn from experimental data.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure:**\nIn the context of random effects models, the general structure can be expressed as:\n\\[ Y_{ij} = \\mu + \\alpha_i + \\beta_j + \\epsilon_{ij} \\]\nwhere \\( Y_{ij} \\) is the response variable, \\( \\mu \\) is the overall mean, \\( \\alpha_i \\) represents the random effects associated with the \\( i \\)-th level of a factor, \\( \\beta_j \\) denotes fixed effects, and \\( \\epsilon_{ij} \\) is the random error term. This model allows for the partitioning of variance into components attributable to different sources.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nThe design layout for studies utilizing Bennett and Franklin's methodologies often involves a hierarchical structure where random effects are nested within fixed effects. This is particularly relevant in multi-level or nested designs, where observations are grouped by different levels of a factor.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nKey assumptions for the application of random effects models include:\n1. Independence of observations.\n2. Normally distributed random effects and errors.\n3. Homogeneity of variance across groups.\n4. The random effects are drawn from a normal distribution.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nBennett and Franklin's approaches are applicable in scenarios where:\n- The experimental units are not independent, and there is a need to account for random variability.\n- Data arise from hierarchical or nested designs.\n- Researchers aim to make inferences about a population based on a finite sample.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis typically involves fitting a mixed-effects model using software that supports such statistical techniques (e.g., R, SAS, or SPSS). The analysis includes estimating fixed and random effects, assessing model fit, and conducting hypothesis tests to evaluate the significance of effects.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- Provides a robust framework for analyzing data with complex structures.\n- Allows for the incorporation of both fixed and random effects, enhancing model flexibility.\n- Facilitates the understanding of variability at different levels of the experimental design.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- Requires careful consideration of model assumptions; violations can lead to incorrect inferences.\n- More complex to implement and interpret compared to simpler ANOVA models.\n- Computationally intensive, especially with large datasets or complex models.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nBennett and Franklin's work is related to other mixed-effects modeling approaches, such as hierarchical linear models and generalized linear mixed models (GLMMs). Alternatives include traditional ANOVA methods when the assumptions of random effects models are not met.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nResearchers should ensure adequate sample sizes to support the estimation of random effects and consider the design's hierarchical structure when planning experiments. It is also crucial to conduct diagnostic checks on model assumptions post-analysis.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nCommon statistical software packages such as R (using the `lme4` or `nlme` packages), SAS (using PROC MIXED), and SPSS (using the Mixed Models procedure) are equipped to handle the analysis of random effects models as discussed by Bennett and Franklin.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nBennett and Franklin's methodologies are widely applied in fields such as agriculture, psychology, and biomedical research, where experiments often involve nested designs or repeated measures. For instance, in agricultural studies, random effects models can be used to analyze crop yields across different plots and treatments while accounting for variability due to environmental factors.\",3\r\n2620,TWO-FACTOR RANDOM MODEL,\"### Two-Factor Random Model\n\nThe Two-Factor Random Model is a statistical framework utilized in mixed model analysis of variance (ANOVA) that incorporates both fixed and random effects associated with two independent variables, or factors. This model is particularly useful in situations where one factor is considered fixed (e.g., treatment levels) and the other is treated as random (e.g., subjects or experimental units drawn from a larger population). The primary purpose of this model is to analyze the variability in response data that can be attributed to both fixed and random factors, thereby providing a more nuanced understanding of the effects at play.\n\n#### Mathematical Formulation\nThe general structure of the Two-Factor Random Model can be expressed as:\n\n\\[ Y_{ijk} = \\mu + \\alpha_i + b_j + \\epsilon_{ijk} \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( Y_{ijk} \\) is the response variable,\n- \\( \\mu \\) is the overall mean,\n- \\( \\alpha_i \\) represents the fixed effect of the \\( i \\)-th level of the fixed factor,\n- \\( b_j \\) denotes the random effect of the \\( j \\)-th level of the random factor (assumed to be normally distributed with mean 0 and variance \\( \\sigma^2_b \\)),\n- \\( \\epsilon_{ijk} \\) is the random error term (also assumed to be normally distributed with mean 0 and variance \\( \\sigma^2_e \\)).\n\n#### Design Layout\nIn a Two-Factor Random Model, the experimental design typically involves a factorial arrangement where one factor is fixed and the other is random. The layout can be structured as a balanced or unbalanced design, depending on the number of levels and the allocation of experimental units to each treatment combination.\n\n#### Assumptions\nFor the validity of the Two-Factor Random Model, several assumptions must be met:\n1. The random effects are normally distributed.\n2. The random effects are independent of the fixed effects.\n3. The residuals (errors) are normally distributed and homoscedastic (constant variance).\n4. The observations are independent.\n\n#### When to Use / Applicability\nThe Two-Factor Random Model is applicable in scenarios where researchers are interested in understanding the effects of a fixed treatment while accounting for random variability introduced by another factor. It is particularly useful in agricultural experiments, clinical trials, and any study where subjects or experimental units are randomly selected from a larger population.\n\n#### Analysis Procedure\nThe analysis typically involves fitting the model using software that supports mixed models (e.g., R, SAS, SPSS). The analysis of variance (ANOVA) is then conducted to assess the significance of the fixed effects while accounting for the random effects. The results can be interpreted through F-tests for fixed effects and variance components for random effects.\n\n#### Advantages and Strengths\n- The Two-Factor Random Model allows for the separation of variability due to fixed and random factors, providing a clearer understanding of the data.\n- It can handle unbalanced designs and missing data more effectively than traditional ANOVA.\n- The model is flexible and can be extended to include additional factors or covariates.\n\n#### Limitations and Disadvantages\n- The complexity of the model can make interpretation challenging, especially for those unfamiliar with mixed models.\n- The assumptions regarding normality and independence must be carefully checked, as violations can lead to incorrect conclusions.\n- The model may require larger sample sizes to accurately estimate variance components.\n\n#### Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives\nRelated designs include the Two-Factor Fixed Model, which does not incorporate random effects, and other mixed models that may include more than two factors or additional covariates. Alternatives such as Generalized Linear Models (GLMs) may be considered when the response variable does not meet the assumptions of normality.\n\n#### Practical Implementation Considerations\nWhen implementing the Two-Factor Random Model, researchers should ensure that the design is adequately powered to detect the effects of interest. Careful planning of the randomization process and consideration of the random effects structure are crucial for valid results.\n\n#### Software Implementation\nCommon statistical software packages such as R (using the `lme4` or `nlme` packages), SAS (using PROC MIXED), and SPSS (using the Mixed Models procedure) provide tools for fitting Two-Factor Random Models and conducting the necessary analyses.\n\n#### Common Applications or Examples\nThe Two-Factor Random Model is frequently applied in fields such as agriculture (e.g., studying the effects of different fertilizers across various plots), psychology (e.g., assessing treatment effects across different groups of subjects), and clinical research (e.g., evaluating drug efficacy across different patient populations).\",8\r\n2630,EXPECTED MEAN SQUARES,\"**Expected Mean Squares**\n\n**Definition and Purpose**: Expected Mean Squares (EMS) are statistical estimates utilized in the context of Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) to evaluate the variance components associated with different sources of variation in an experimental design. They serve as a critical component in determining the appropriate test statistics for hypothesis testing, particularly in comparing variances across different groups or treatments.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation**: The Expected Mean Squares are derived from the variance components of the model and are typically expressed in terms of the parameters of the underlying statistical model. For a general linear model, the expected mean square for a factor can be represented as:\n\n\\[\nEMS = \\sigma^2 + n \\cdot \\theta\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\sigma^2\\) is the error variance, \\(n\\) is the number of observations per group, and \\(\\theta\\) represents the effect size associated with the factor.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**: In the context of experimental designs, the layout of expected mean squares is often presented in an ANOVA table, where each source of variation (e.g., treatments, interactions, error) has an associated expected mean square value. This table facilitates the comparison of variances across different groups.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**: The validity of expected mean squares relies on several key assumptions:\n1. Independence of observations.\n2. Normality of the residuals.\n3. Homogeneity of variances across groups (homoscedasticity).\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**: Expected mean squares are applicable in any scenario where ANOVA is employed to compare means across multiple groups. They are particularly useful when the researcher needs to assess the significance of different factors in a factorial design or when analyzing variance in a randomized block design.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**: The analysis procedure involving expected mean squares typically includes:\n1. Formulating the null and alternative hypotheses.\n2. Calculating the expected mean squares for each source of variation.\n3. Computing the F-statistic by taking the ratio of the expected mean squares of the treatment to the expected mean squares of the error.\n4. Comparing the calculated F-statistic to the critical value from the F-distribution to determine significance.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**: The use of expected mean squares provides several advantages:\n- They allow for a clear understanding of the contribution of each factor to the overall variance.\n- They facilitate the identification of significant effects in complex experimental designs.\n- They are essential for constructing valid test statistics in ANOVA.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**: Despite their strengths, expected mean squares have limitations:\n- They are sensitive to violations of the underlying assumptions, particularly normality and homogeneity of variances.\n- Misinterpretation can occur if the expected mean squares are not correctly calculated or if the model is misspecified.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**: Expected mean squares are closely related to other statistical methods such as regression analysis and mixed models. Alternatives to ANOVA, such as non-parametric tests (e.g., Kruskal-Wallis test), may be considered when assumptions of ANOVA are not met.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**: When implementing expected mean squares in practice, researchers should ensure that the assumptions of the ANOVA are thoroughly checked. It is also important to have a sufficient sample size to achieve reliable estimates of variance components.\n\n**Software Implementation**: Many statistical software packages, such as R, SAS, and SPSS, provide built-in functions to calculate expected mean squares as part of their ANOVA procedures. Users can easily generate ANOVA tables that include expected mean squares for their experimental designs.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**: Expected mean squares are commonly used in various fields, including agriculture (e.g., comparing crop yields across different treatments), psychology (e.g., assessing the effects of different interventions), and industrial experiments (e.g., evaluating the performance of different manufacturing processes).\",11\r\n2635,TWO-FACTOR FIXED EFFECTS MODEL,\"The two-factor fixed effects model is a statistical model used to analyze the effects of two independent variables on a dependent variable, assuming fixed effects.\",6\r\n141,MIXED MODEL,\"### Mixed Model\n\nA mixed model is a statistical model that integrates both fixed and random effects, making it a versatile tool in the analysis of complex data structures. The primary purpose of a mixed model is to account for variability at different levels of data, which is particularly useful in hierarchical or grouped data scenarios, such as those encountered in longitudinal studies or experiments with repeated measures.\n\n#### Mathematical Formulation\nThe general structure of a mixed model can be expressed as:\n\n\\[ Y_{ij} = \\mu + \\beta X_{ij} + u_j + \\epsilon_{ij} \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( Y_{ij} \\) is the response variable for the \\( i \\)-th observation in the \\( j \\)-th group,\n- \\( \\mu \\) is the overall mean,\n- \\( \\beta X_{ij} \\) represents the fixed effects associated with the predictors \\( X \\),\n- \\( u_j \\) is the random effect associated with the \\( j \\)-th group (assumed to be normally distributed with mean 0 and variance \\( \\sigma^2_u \\)),\n- \\( \\epsilon_{ij} \\) is the residual error (also assumed to be normally distributed with mean 0 and variance \\( \\sigma^2_\\epsilon \\)).\n\n#### Design Layout\nIn experimental contexts, such as the Gauge R&R (Repeatability and Reproducibility) experiment, mixed models can be structured to analyze data collected from multiple operators, instruments, or conditions, where some factors are fixed (e.g., specific operators) and others are random (e.g., variability among instruments).\n\n#### Assumptions\nFor the validity of mixed models, several assumptions must be met:\n1. The random effects are normally distributed.\n2. The residuals are normally distributed and independent.\n3. Homogeneity of variance across groups.\n4. The fixed effects are linearly related to the response variable.\n\n#### Applicability and Selection Criteria\nMixed models are particularly applicable when:\n- Data is collected in a hierarchical or nested structure.\n- There are repeated measures on the same subjects or experimental units.\n- The researcher is interested in both population-level effects (fixed effects) and individual-level variability (random effects).\n\n#### Analysis Procedure\nThe analysis of mixed models typically involves:\n1. Specification of the model structure (identifying fixed and random effects).\n2. Estimation of parameters using methods such as Restricted Maximum Likelihood (REML) or Maximum Likelihood (ML).\n3. Hypothesis testing for fixed effects, often using F-tests or t-tests.\n4. Evaluation of model fit and assumptions through residual analysis.\n\n#### Advantages and Strengths\n- Mixed models can handle unbalanced data and missing values effectively.\n- They provide a more accurate representation of data that exhibit both fixed and random variability.\n- They allow for the analysis of complex experimental designs without losing degrees of freedom.\n\n#### Limitations and Disadvantages\n- The complexity of mixed models can make them difficult to specify and interpret.\n- They require larger sample sizes to accurately estimate random effects.\n- Model convergence issues may arise, particularly with complex structures.\n\n#### Related Designs and Alternatives\nMixed models are related to other statistical methods such as Generalized Linear Mixed Models (GLMMs) and Hierarchical Linear Models (HLMs). Alternatives include traditional ANOVA methods, which may not adequately account for random effects.\n\n#### Practical Implementation Considerations\nWhen implementing mixed models, researchers should consider:\n- The choice of software for analysis, with popular options including R (using packages like `lme4`), SAS, and SPSS.\n- The need for careful model specification to ensure that both fixed and random effects are appropriately included.\n\n#### Software Implementation\nCommon software implementations for mixed models include:\n- R: The `lme4` package for linear mixed-effects models.\n- SAS: PROC MIXED for fitting mixed models.\n- SPSS: Mixed Models procedure for analyzing data with both fixed and random effects.\n\n#### Common Applications or Examples\nMixed models are widely used in various fields, including:\n- Biomedical research for analyzing clinical trial data with repeated measures.\n- Agricultural studies to evaluate the effects of different treatments across various plots.\n- Social sciences for analyzing survey data where responses are nested within groups (e.g., students within schools).\n\nIn summary, mixed models serve as a powerful analytical tool in the Design of Experiments and Analysis of Variance, allowing researchers to effectively model and interpret data that exhibit both fixed and random variability.\",5\r\n1860,TWO-FACTOR INTERACTION,\"### Two-Factor Interaction: Comprehensive Summary\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nTwo-factor interaction refers to the situation in which the effect of one independent variable (factor) on a dependent variable (response) is influenced by the level of another independent variable. This concept is crucial in the context of factorial experiments, where multiple factors are studied simultaneously to understand their individual and combined effects on the response variable.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure**  \nIn a factorial design, the model for a two-factor interaction can be expressed as follows:\n\n\\[ Y_{ijk} = \\mu + \\alpha_i + \\beta_j + (\\alpha\\beta)_{ij} + \\epsilon_{ijk} \\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\( Y_{ijk} \\) is the response variable.\n- \\( \\mu \\) is the overall mean.\n- \\( \\alpha_i \\) is the effect of the \\( i \\)-th level of factor A.\n- \\( \\beta_j \\) is the effect of the \\( j \\)-th level of factor B.\n- \\( (\\alpha\\beta)_{ij} \\) is the interaction effect between factors A and B.\n- \\( \\epsilon_{ijk} \\) is the random error term.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nIn a two-factor factorial design, all possible combinations of the levels of the two factors are tested. For example, if factor A has \\( a \\) levels and factor B has \\( b \\) levels, the design will consist of \\( a \\times b \\) treatment combinations. Each combination is typically replicated to ensure the reliability of the results.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nThe validity of the two-factor interaction analysis relies on several assumptions:\n1. Independence of observations: The responses must be independent of each other.\n2. Normality: The residuals (differences between observed and predicted values) should be normally distributed.\n3. Homogeneity of variance: The variance among the groups should be approximately equal.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nTwo-factor interaction analysis is applicable when researchers are interested in understanding how two factors simultaneously influence a response variable. It is particularly useful when:\n- The interaction between factors is suspected or of interest.\n- The experimental design allows for the examination of multiple factors at once.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis of two-factor interactions typically involves:\n1. Conducting an ANOVA to test for significant main effects and interaction effects.\n2. If significant interactions are found, further analysis (such as simple effects analysis) may be performed to explore the nature of the interaction.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \n- Provides a comprehensive understanding of how factors interact, leading to more informed decision-making.\n- Efficiently utilizes resources by examining multiple factors simultaneously.\n- Can reveal complex relationships that may not be apparent when factors are studied in isolation.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \n- Increased complexity in analysis and interpretation, especially with higher-order interactions.\n- Requires a larger sample size to maintain statistical power, particularly when examining interactions.\n- May lead to confounding if not properly designed, especially in the presence of unmeasured variables.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nTwo-factor interaction is often studied within the framework of factorial designs, including:\n- Full factorial designs: All possible combinations of factors are tested.\n- Fractional factorial designs: A subset of combinations is tested to reduce resource requirements while still allowing for interaction analysis.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing a two-factor interaction study, researchers should consider:\n- The number of levels for each factor and the total number of experimental runs required.\n- The need for randomization to mitigate bias.\n- The potential for confounding variables that may affect the results.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nStatistical software packages such as R, SAS, and SPSS provide tools for conducting ANOVA and analyzing two-factor interactions. These tools often include functions for visualizing interaction plots, which can aid in interpreting the results.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nTwo-factor interaction analysis is commonly used in various fields, including:\n- Agriculture: Studying the interaction between different fertilizers and irrigation levels on crop yield.\n- Manufacturing: Analyzing the effects of temperature and pressure on product quality.\n- Psychology: Investigating how different teaching methods and student engagement levels affect learning outcomes. \n\nIn summary, two-factor interaction is a fundamental concept in the Design of Experiments that allows researchers to explore the combined effects of two factors on a response variable, providing insights that can lead to more effective experimental designs and better decision-making.\",2\r\n2638,THREE-FACTOR INTERACTION,Three-factor interaction refers to the combined effect of three factors on the response variable in the factorial experiment.,1\r\n1026,SEARLE,\"**Comprehensive Summary of Searle in Statistical Analysis**\n\nSearle refers to a prominent statistician recognized for his significant contributions to the fields of linear models and mixed models within statistics. His work has been influential in the development and application of statistical methodologies, particularly in the context of variance components and their analysis.\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nSearle's contributions primarily focus on the formulation and application of linear and mixed models, which are essential for analyzing data that may exhibit complex structures, such as hierarchical or nested designs. These models allow researchers to account for both fixed and random effects, facilitating a more nuanced understanding of variability in experimental data.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation:**\nIn the context of linear models, the general form can be expressed as:\n\\[ Y = X\\beta + \\epsilon \\]\nwhere \\( Y \\) is the vector of observed responses, \\( X \\) is the design matrix, \\( \\beta \\) represents the vector of fixed effects, and \\( \\epsilon \\) is the vector of random errors. For mixed models, the formulation extends to include random effects, typically represented as:\n\\[ Y = X\\beta + Zb + \\epsilon \\]\nwhere \\( Z \\) is the design matrix for random effects and \\( b \\) is the vector of random effects.\n\n**Design Layout:**\nSearle's work is particularly relevant in the context of experimental designs that require the analysis of variance components, such as split-plot designs or hierarchical models. These designs allow for the investigation of multiple sources of variability and their interactions.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of Searle's models relies on several key assumptions:\n1. The residuals are normally distributed.\n2. Homoscedasticity (constant variance) of residuals across levels of the independent variables.\n3. Independence of observations.\n4. Correct specification of the model, including the appropriate inclusion of fixed and random effects.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nSearle's methodologies are applicable in scenarios where data exhibit hierarchical structures or when random effects are present. Researchers should consider using these models when dealing with repeated measures, multi-level data, or when the experimental design involves both fixed and random factors.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis typically involves estimating the parameters of the model using methods such as Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE) or Restricted Maximum Likelihood (REML). Hypothesis testing can be conducted using F-tests or likelihood ratio tests to assess the significance of fixed effects and variance components.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\nThe strengths of Searle's contributions include:\n- The ability to model complex data structures effectively.\n- Flexibility in accommodating both fixed and random effects.\n- Enhanced understanding of variance components, leading to better insights into the sources of variability.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\nHowever, there are limitations associated with these models:\n- Complexity in model specification and interpretation, particularly for non-statisticians.\n- Computational intensity, especially for large datasets or complex models.\n- Potential for overfitting if random effects are not appropriately specified.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nSearle's work is closely related to other statistical methodologies, including Generalized Linear Models (GLMs) and Bayesian hierarchical models. Alternatives may include simpler ANOVA designs when the assumptions of mixed models are not met.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing Searle's models, researchers should ensure proper data preparation, including checking for outliers and verifying assumptions. Model diagnostics are crucial to validate the fit and appropriateness of the chosen model.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nSearle's methodologies can be implemented using various statistical software packages, including R (with packages such as `lme4` for mixed models), SAS, and SPSS, which provide functions for fitting linear and mixed models.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nCommon applications of Searle's contributions include agricultural experiments, clinical trials, and social science research, where data often exhibit hierarchical structures or require the analysis of variance components to understand the effects of different factors.\n\nIn summary, Searle's work in linear and mixed models provides a robust framework for analyzing complex data structures, making significant contributions to the field of statistics and its applications in various research domains.\",4\r\n2627,RESTRICTED MODEL,\"### Comprehensive Summary of the Restricted Model\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nThe restricted model is a type of mixed model used in statistical analysis, particularly in the context of Design of Experiments (DOE) and Analysis of Variance (ANOVA). It serves to impose specific assumptions on the data, allowing for a more structured approach to modeling complex relationships. This model is slightly more general than the unrestricted model, as it accommodates both positive and negative covariances among the random effects.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure**  \nThe restricted model can be expressed in the general form:  \n\\[ Y = X\\beta + Z\\gamma + \\epsilon \\]  \nwhere \\( Y \\) is the response variable, \\( X \\) is the design matrix for fixed effects, \\( \\beta \\) represents the fixed effects coefficients, \\( Z \\) is the design matrix for random effects, \\( \\gamma \\) denotes the random effects, and \\( \\epsilon \\) is the error term. The restrictions imposed on the model typically relate to the covariance structure of the random effects, which can be specified to reflect prior knowledge or theoretical considerations.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nIn the context of experimental design, the restricted model is often applied in mixed-effects frameworks where both fixed and random factors are present. The layout may involve multiple levels of factors, with random effects accounting for variability across experimental units or groups.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nKey assumptions for the restricted model include:\n1. The random effects are normally distributed.\n2. The residuals are independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) with a mean of zero.\n3. The covariance structure specified in the model accurately reflects the underlying data relationships.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nThe restricted model is particularly useful when researchers have prior knowledge about the relationships among variables or when certain constraints need to be imposed on the model. It is applicable in scenarios where both fixed and random effects are present, and where the researcher aims to control for specific sources of variability.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis typically involves fitting the restricted model to the data using maximum likelihood estimation or restricted maximum likelihood (REML). Model diagnostics and goodness-of-fit assessments are essential to validate the assumptions and the appropriateness of the model.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \nThe restricted model offers several advantages:\n- It allows for the incorporation of prior knowledge through the imposition of restrictions.\n- It can provide more accurate estimates of fixed effects by controlling for random variability.\n- The flexibility in modeling covariances enhances the model's applicability to complex data structures.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \nDespite its strengths, the restricted model has limitations:\n- The imposition of restrictions may lead to biased estimates if the assumptions are incorrect.\n- It can be computationally intensive, especially with large datasets or complex covariance structures.\n- The interpretation of results may become complicated due to the added constraints.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nRelated methodologies include the unrestricted model, which does not impose such restrictions, and other mixed models that may use different covariance structures. Alternative approaches may involve simpler linear models or generalized linear models, depending on the nature of the data.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing the restricted model, researchers should carefully consider the choice of restrictions based on theoretical foundations or empirical evidence. It is also crucial to conduct thorough model diagnostics to ensure the validity of the assumptions.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nThe restricted model can be implemented in various statistical software packages, including R (using packages like `lme4` or `nlme`), SAS, and SPSS, which provide functions for fitting mixed models with specified covariance structures.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nCommon applications of the restricted model include longitudinal data analysis, hierarchical data structures, and experiments where random effects are expected to influence the response variable, such as in agricultural studies, clinical trials, and educational research.\",4\r\n2609,RANDOM FACTORS,\"**Comprehensive Summary of Random Factors**\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nRandom factors are variables in an experimental design that are randomly assigned to experimental units, introducing variability into the results. They are essential in mixed models, where they account for random variation that can affect the outcome of an experiment. The purpose of incorporating random factors is to capture the inherent variability in the data that is not explained by fixed factors, thereby improving the generalizability of the results.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure**  \nIn a mixed-effects model, the structure can be represented as:  \n\\[ Y_{ij} = \\mu + \\alpha_i + b_j + \\epsilon_{ij} \\]  \nwhere \\( Y_{ij} \\) is the response variable, \\( \\mu \\) is the overall mean, \\( \\alpha_i \\) represents the fixed effects, \\( b_j \\) denotes the random effects associated with the random factor, and \\( \\epsilon_{ij} \\) is the residual error. The random effects \\( b_j \\) are typically assumed to be normally distributed with a mean of zero and a certain variance.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nIn experimental designs that incorporate random factors, the layout often includes a combination of fixed and random factors. For example, in a randomized block design, blocks may represent random factors, while treatments are fixed factors. The random factors are assigned to experimental units in a way that allows for the estimation of their variance components.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nThe validity of models incorporating random factors relies on several assumptions:\n1. The random effects are normally distributed.\n2. The random effects are independent of the fixed effects.\n3. The residuals are normally distributed and homoscedastic (constant variance).\n4. The random factors are appropriately specified in the model.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nRandom factors are particularly useful in situations where the experimental units are not homogeneous, and there is a need to account for variability due to random effects. They are applicable in hierarchical or nested designs, where the random factors can represent different levels of variability (e.g., different batches, operators, or locations). Selection criteria include the nature of the experimental units and the need to generalize findings beyond the specific levels of fixed factors.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis of data involving random factors typically involves mixed-effects models or hierarchical linear models. The analysis can be performed using software packages that support these models, such as R (using the `lme4` or `nlme` packages), SAS, or SPSS. The analysis includes estimating variance components, testing hypotheses about fixed effects, and assessing the significance of random effects.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \nThe incorporation of random factors allows for a more realistic representation of variability in experimental data. It enhances the generalizability of results by accounting for random effects that may influence the outcome. Additionally, it can lead to more efficient estimates of fixed effects by reducing the residual variance.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \nOne limitation of using random factors is the complexity of the model, which can make interpretation challenging. Additionally, if the random effects are not correctly specified, it can lead to biased estimates and incorrect conclusions. The requirement for larger sample sizes to accurately estimate variance components can also be a drawback.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nRandom factors are often used in conjunction with fixed factors in mixed models. Alternatives include fully random designs or completely randomized designs, depending on the research question and the nature of the experimental units. Other related methods include Bayesian hierarchical models, which also account for random effects.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing random factors in an experiment, careful consideration should be given to the selection of random effects and their levels. It is crucial to ensure that the random factors are relevant to the research question and that the experimental design is adequately powered to detect the effects of interest.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nCommon statistical software for analyzing models with random factors includes R (with packages like `lme4`), SAS (using PROC MIXED), and SPSS (using mixed models procedures). These tools provide functionalities for fitting mixed models and conducting hypothesis tests related to both fixed and random effects.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nRandom factors are frequently used in agricultural experiments, clinical trials, and industrial studies where variability due to different operators, machines, or environmental conditions is expected. For instance, in a clinical trial, patients may be considered a random factor to account for variability in responses due to individual differences.\",4\r\n2634,CORNFIELD AND TUKEY,Cornfield and Tukey are statisticians recognized for their work in statistical theory and methodology.,2\r\n\n\n-----Relationships-----\nhuman_readable_id,source,target,description,combined_degree\r\n3652,RANDOM EFFECT,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,Random effects are included in analysis of variance to account for variability among subjects or experimental units,265\r\n3676,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,FIXED EFFECTS,Fixed effects are analyzed within the context of the Analysis of Variance,264\r\n1200,BENNETT AND FRANKLIN,RANDOM EFFECTS MODEL,Bennett and Franklin discuss the finite population case in relation to the Random Effects Model,23\r\n3690,TWO-FACTOR RANDOM MODEL,EXPECTED MEAN SQUARES,Expected mean squares are also relevant in the analysis of the two-factor random model,19\r\n3688,TWO-FACTOR FIXED EFFECTS MODEL,EXPECTED MEAN SQUARES,Expected mean squares are calculated as part of the analysis in the two-factor fixed effects model,17\r\n3668,EXPECTED MEAN SQUARES,MIXED MODEL,Expected mean squares are derived from mixed models to assess variance,16\r\n3699,TWO-FACTOR INTERACTION,EXPECTED MEAN SQUARES,Two-factor interaction is analyzed through expected mean squares in the context of the factorial experiment,13\r\n3700,THREE-FACTOR INTERACTION,EXPECTED MEAN SQUARES,Three-factor interaction is analyzed through expected mean squares in the context of the factorial experiment,12\r\n3686,SEARLE,TWO-FACTOR FIXED EFFECTS MODEL,Searle's work on linear models is relevant to the development of the two-factor fixed effects model,10\r\n3660,MIXED MODEL,RESTRICTED MODEL,The restricted model is a specific application of the mixed model in the analysis,9\r\n3670,RANDOM FACTORS,MIXED MODEL,\"Random factors are a key component of mixed models, influencing the variability of outcomes\",9\r\n3684,CORNFIELD AND TUKEY,TWO-FACTOR FIXED EFFECTS MODEL,Cornfield and Tukey's contributions to statistical theory support the application of the two-factor fixed effects model,8\r\n\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its entities.\n- IMPACT SEVERITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation of the IMPACT severity rating.\n- DETAILED FINDINGS: A list of 5-10 key insights about the community. Each insight should have a short summary followed by multiple paragraphs of explanatory text grounded according to the grounding rules below. Be comprehensive.\n\nReturn output as a well-formed JSON-formatted string with the following format:\n    {\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\nOutput:", "role": "user"}], "parameters": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "frequency_penalty": 0.0, "max_tokens": 4000, "n": 1, "presence_penalty": 0.0, "temperature": 0.0, "top_p": 1.0, "response_format": {"type": "json_object"}}}, "key": "chat_create_community_report_ae9c12dc1e1537ae02f10abded10ed262c7ab2fa1227f8067173bf6995685650_v2"}